          "model_output": "To find the percent decrease in the area of the square, we first need to determine the area of the square. The area of a square is given by the formula $A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height}$, where $\\text{base}$ and $\\text{height}$ are the lengths of the two opposite sides of the square.\n\nGiven that the lengths of the two opposite sides are decreased by $40\\%$ and the lengths of the other two sides are increased by $50\\%$, we can set up the following equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} + \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} + \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can solve for $\\text{base}$:\n\n\\[\n\\text{base} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} + \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{base} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find the percent decrease in the area of the square:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Percent decrease} = \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the percent decrease in the area of the square is $\\boxed{\\frac{3}{4}}$.",
