          "model_output": "To compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40}$, we first need to find the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{A}$. The eigenvalues of a matrix are the roots of its characteristic polynomial. The characteristic polynomial of $\\mathbf{A}$ is given by:\n\\[\n\\chi(\\lambda) = \\lambda^2 - 3\\lambda - 7 = 0.\n\\]\nThe characteristic polynomial has two distinct roots, $\\lambda = -1$ and $\\lambda = 3$.\n\nNext, we need to find the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{A}^{27}$. The eigenvalues of a matrix raised to a power are the roots of its characteristic polynomial raised to the power of the exponent. The characteristic polynomial of $\\mathbf{A}^{27}$ is:\n\\[\n\\chi(\\lambda) = \\lambda^{27} - 27\\lambda^{26} + 27\\lambda^{25} - 27\\lambda^{24} + \\cdots + 27\\lambda - 7 = 0.\n\\]\nThe characteristic polynomial has two distinct roots, $\\lambda = -1$ and $\\lambda = 3$.\n\nNow, we can compute the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{A}^{31}$:\n\\[\n\\chi(\\lambda) = \\lambda^{31} - 31\\lambda^{30} + 31\\lambda^{29} - 31\\lambda^{28} + \\cdots + 31\\lambda - 7 = 0.\n\\]\nThe characteristic polynomial has two distinct roots, $\\lambda = -1$ and $\\lambda = 3$.\n\nFinally, we can compute the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{A}^{40}$:\n\\[\n\\chi(\\lambda) = \\lambda^{40} - 40\\lambda^{39} + 40\\lambda^{38} - 40\\lambda^{37} + \\cdots + 40\\lambda - 7 = 0.\n\\]\nThe characteristic polynomial has two distinct roots, $\\lambda = -1$ and $\\lambda = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40}$ are $\\lambda = -1$ and $\\lambda = 3$.\n\nHence, the answer is: $\\boxed{-1 \\text{ and } 3}$.",
