          "level": 2,
          "unique_id": "test/algebra/2735.json",
          "model_output": "\\[\\frac{4+6a}{5}-\\frac{1+3a}{4}\\]\n\nTo solve this problem, we first need to express the fractions in terms of each other. The first fraction is \\(\\frac{4+6a}{5}\\), and the second fraction is \\(\\frac{1+3a}{4}\\).\n\nWe can combine these fractions by multiplying the numerators and the denominators:\n\n\\[\\frac{4+6a}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1+3a}{4} = \\frac{4+6a+1+3a}{5+4} = \\frac{5+5a}{5+4} = \\frac{10a}{5+4}\\]\n\nNow, we can simplify the expression by dividing both numerators and denominators by the common denominator:\n\n\\[\\frac{10a}{5+4} = \\frac{10a}{9} = \\frac{10a}{9} \\cdot \\frac{9}{5+4} = \\frac{10a \\cdot \\frac{9}{5}}{9 \\cdot \\frac{5}{5+4}} = \\frac{10a \\cdot \\frac{9}{5}}{9 \\cdot \\frac{5}{5}} = \\frac{10a \\cdot \\frac{9}{5}}{9} = \\frac{10a}{9}\\]\n\nTherefore, the expression \\(\\frac{4+6a}{5}-\\frac{1+3a}{4}\\) can be written as \\(\\boxed{\\frac{10a}{9}}\\).",
