          "model_output": "To solve the problem, we start by finding the maximum and minimum values of the quadratic polynomial $P(x)$ using the given inequality.\n\n1. **Find the maximum value of $P(x)$:**\n   - Since $P(x)$ is a quadratic polynomial with real coefficients, its maximum value is achieved when the coefficients are equal.\n   - Substituting the given inequality into the quadratic formula, we get:\n     \\[\n     P(x) = \\frac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a}\n     \\]\n   - Solving for $a$, we find:\n     \\[\n     a = \\frac{b^2 - 4ac}{4}\n     \\]\n   - Substituting $a = \\frac{b^2 - 4ac}{4}$ into the quadratic formula, we get:\n     \\[\n     P(x) = \\frac{b^2 - 4(b^2 - 4ac)}{4}\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     P(x) = \\frac{b^2 - 4ac}{4}\n     \\]\n   - Since $b^2 - 4ac \\geq 0$, the maximum value of $P(x)$ is achieved when $b^2 - 4ac = 0$.\n\n2. **Find the minimum value of $P(x)$:**\n   - Since $P(x)$ is a quadratic polynomial with real coefficients, its minimum value is achieved when the coefficients are equal.\n   - Substituting the given inequality into the quadratic formula, we get:\n     \\[\n     P(x) = \\frac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a}\n     \\]\n   - Solving for $a$, we find:\n     \\[\n     a = \\frac{b^2 - 4ac}{4}\n     \\]\n   - Substituting $a = \\frac{b^2 - 4ac}{4}$ into the quadratic formula, we get:\n     \\[\n     P(x) = \\frac{b^2 - 4(b^2 - 4ac)}{4}\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     P(x) = \\frac{b^2 - 4ac}{4}\n     \\]\n   - Since $b^2 - 4ac \\geq 0$, the minimum value of $P(x)$ is achieved when $b^2 - 4ac = 0$.\n\n3. **Combine the results:**\n   - The maximum value of $P(x)$ is $\\boxed{181}$.\n   - The minimum value of $P(x)$ is $\\boxed{181}$.\n\nConclusion:\nThe maximum value of the quadratic polynomial $P(x)$ is $\\boxed{181}$, and the minimum value is $\\boxed{181}$.",
